          "source": "def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the energy-vector, metric, and unit conventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. A rest-energy parameter $\\mathsf M\\geq0$ and every energy-vector component are measured in joules. Set $D_{\\mathsf M}=\\mathbb R^3$ when $\\mathsf M>0$ and $D_0=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Define $\\iota_{\\mathsf M}:D_{\\mathsf M}\\to\\mathbb R^4$ by\n$$\\iota_{\\mathsf M}(\\mathbf p)=\\bigl(\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+\\mathsf M^2},\\mathbf p\\bigr), \\qquad \\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}:=\\iota_{\\mathsf M}(D_{\\mathsf M}).$$\nThus $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}$ is the positive-energy shell, with its subspace Borel structure. Define the Borel shell measure $\\nu_{\\mathsf M}$ as the pushforward under $\\iota_{\\mathsf M}$ of the measure absolutely continuous with respect to three-dimensional Lebesgue measure on $D_{\\mathsf M}$, with density $((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+\\mathsf M^2})^{-1}$. In particular, the massless apex is excluded.\n\nLet $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$ and let $s>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$. Define\n$$k_*=\\frac{\\sqrt{[s-(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2][s-(\\mathsf M_1-\\mathsf M_2)^2]}}{2\\sqrt{s}}, \\qquad E_i^*=\\sqrt{k_*^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}.$$\nBoth factors under the square root are positive, so $k_*>0$. On the Euclidean unit sphere $S^2$, let $\\Omega$ be the chart surface Borel measure of [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]. Define the finite Borel measure $\\Phi_2$ on $S^2$ and the shell-pair map $\\Psi_2:S^2\\to\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2}$ by\n$$d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{16\\pi^2\\sqrt{s}}\\,d\\Omega, \\qquad \\Psi_2(\\mathbf n)=((E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)).$$\nThe center-of-momentum measure on the shell-pair space is the pushforward $(\\Psi_2)_\\#\\Phi_2$. The angular measure $\\Phi_2$ and its shell-pair pushforward are distinct typed measures.\n\nFor incoming energy four-vectors $p_i\\in\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}}$, where $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ for $i=1,2$, define the invariant flux when its radicand is nonnegative by\n$$F(p_1,p_2)=4\\sqrt{(p_1\\cdot p_2)^2-\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}}^2\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}^2}, \\qquad p\\cdot q=h(p,q).$$\nLet $\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ and $\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}$ be finite nonempty sets of initial and final spin labels, respectively, and let $w_i\\geq0$ for $i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ satisfy $\\sum_iw_i=1$. A spin-resolved formal amplitude is a family of dimensionless Borel functions $\\mathcal A_{if}:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$, indexed by $i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ and $f\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}$. Define the Borel function\n$$\\overline{|\\mathcal A(\\mathbf n)|^2}:=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}}w_i\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}}|\\mathcal A_{if}(\\mathbf n)|^2.$$\nFor a specified final two-particle channel, set $S_f=2$ when its two final particles are identical and $S_f=1$ when they are distinguishable. When the incoming flux is positive, $F>0$, define the angular cross-section coefficient measure $\\sigma_{\\rm coeff}$ on $S^2$ by\n$$d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{F S_f}\\, \\overline{|\\mathcal A(\\mathbf n)|^2}\\,d\\Phi_2.$$\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>0$ and a specified single-channel Borel formal decay amplitude $\\mathcal G:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$ measured in joules, define the extended nonnegative coefficient\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{1}{2\\hbar\\mathsf M_0 S_f} \\int_{S^2}|\\mathcal G(\\mathbf n)|^2\\,d\\Phi_2.$$\nIt is a finite rate coefficient when this integral is finite; otherwise the displayed integral has value $+\\infty$.\nThese are mathematical coefficient maps for the explicitly specified formal amplitudes; their definitions assert no exact interacting scattering operator or decay law.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $m\\ge1$, let U,V be open subsets of $\\mathbb R^m$, and let $T:U\\to V$ be a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Borel $h:V\\to[0,\\infty]$, $\\int_V h(y)\\,dy=\\int_U h(T(x))|\\det DT(x)|\\,dx$, with equality in $[0,\\infty]$ and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. This statement concerns Borel h; no completed-measurable substitution is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and $n\\ge2$. The chart surface measure on $S^{n-1}$ equals the polar measure $\\sigma$. Orthogonal transformations preserve it. The map $\\omega\\mapsto a+R\\omega$, $R>0$, multiplies surface measure by $R^{n-1}$, and $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $a=(a_0,\\mathbf a)$ be any future unit vector, so $a_0>0$ and $a_0^2-|\\mathbf a|^2=1$. If $\\mathbf a=0$, put $B=I$. Otherwise put $r=|\\mathbf a|$, $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf a/r$, and define $B$ to act by $\\begin{pmatrix}a_0&r\\\\r&a_0\\end{pmatrix}$ on $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,\\mathbf n)$ and by the identity on its spatial orthogonal complement. In both cases $B$ preserves $h$, has determinant one, preserves the future cone, and sends $e_0$ to $a$. For any proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$, apply this construction to $a=\\Lambda e_0$; then $B^{-1}\\Lambda$ fixes $e_0$ and restricts to a proper spatial rotation on $e_0^\\perp$. Thus every such $\\Lambda$ is a boost followed by a spatial rotation. The construction also gives, for every future unit vector $a$, an inverse boost taking $a$ to $e_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "A spatial rotation has Jacobian one and leaves $E$ fixed. By step 1.1, it remains to check a boost directed along the first spatial axis. For that boost, $p'_1=a_0p_1+rE$ and $E'=a_0E+rp_1$, so $\\partial p'_1/\\partial p_1=a_0+rp_1/E=E'/E>0$; the other spatial coordinates are unchanged. The inverse boost is obtained by replacing $r$ with $-r$, so this spatial map is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. By [F3], $d^3\\mathbf p'=(E'/E)d^3\\mathbf p$ in every nonnegative Borel integral, and therefore $d^3\\mathbf p'/(2E')=d^3\\mathbf p/(2E)$. This proves shell-measure invariance under the boost and, using the rotation factorization, under every proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply the boost construction in step 1.1 to the future unit vector $P/\\sqrt{s}$; its inverse takes $P/\\sqrt{s}$ to $e_0$. Any two maps taking $P/\\sqrt{s}$ to $e_0$ differ by a Lorentz map fixing $e_0$, hence by a spatial rotation on $e_0^\\perp$; orthogonal invariance in [F4] therefore makes $\\Phi_{2,P}$ independent of the chosen map. Under the chosen map, write $\\widetilde f(k_1,k_2)=f(\\Lambda_P^{-1}k_1,\\Lambda_P^{-1}k_2)$. Step 2.1 preserves each shell measure, and $|\\det\\Lambda_P|=1$ preserves the four-dimensional delta, so the integral reduces to the rest-frame calculation with $\\widetilde f$. Integrating the spatial delta first sets $\\mathbf k_2=-\\mathbf k_1$ and gives the remaining radial-angular measure\n$$\\frac{\\widetilde f((E_1(r),r\\mathbf n),(E_2(r),-r\\mathbf n))}{4(2\\pi)^2E_1(r)E_2(r)}r^2\\,dr\\,d\\Omega\\,\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r)).$$\nHere $d^3\\mathbf k_1=r^2dr\\,d\\Omega$ by [F4].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $r>0$, $E_1(r)+E_2(r)$ is strictly increasing, with derivative $r(1/E_1+1/E_2)>0$. The threshold assumption gives a unique root $k_*>0$. At this root, $E_1+E_2=\\sqrt{s}$ and $E_1-E_2=(\\mathsf M_1^2-\\mathsf M_2^2)/\\sqrt{s}$, so $E_1=(s+\\mathsf M_1^2-\\mathsf M_2^2)/(2\\sqrt{s})$, $E_2=(s-\\mathsf M_1^2+\\mathsf M_2^2)/(2\\sqrt{s})$, and\n$$k_*^2=E_1^2-\\mathsf M_1^2=\\frac{[s-(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2][s-(\\mathsf M_1-\\mathsf M_2)^2]}{4s}.$$\nThe derivative at the root is $k_*\\sqrt{s}/(E_1E_2)$. Applying [F3] to the one-dimensional increasing change of variable in step 3.1, for each fixed $\\mathbf n$, gives the angular density $\\widetilde f((E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n))k_*d\\Omega/(16\\pi^2\\sqrt{s})$. Integrating this nonnegative Borel density in the stipulated order gives exactly $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_{2,P}$ by its pushforward definition. In the center-of-momentum frame, this is the pushforward $(\\Psi_2)_\\#\\Phi_2$ from the Definition, where $\\Phi_2$ is a measure on $S^2$. To compute the angular total, use $X(\\vartheta,\\varphi)=(\\sin\\vartheta\\cos\\varphi,\\sin\\vartheta\\sin\\varphi,\\cos\\vartheta)$ for $0<\\vartheta<\\pi$ and $0<\\varphi<2\\pi$; its chart-area density is $|\\partial_{\\vartheta}X\\times\\partial_{\\varphi}X|=\\sin\\vartheta$. The omitted meridian and poles are null in the two-dimensional chart measure, so $\\Omega(S^2)=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin\\vartheta\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\vartheta=4\\pi$. It follows that $\\Phi_2(S^2)=k_*/(4\\pi\\sqrt{s})$. For equal daughter energies $\\mathsf M$, this gives $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\beta/(8\\pi)$, where $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the incoming center-of-momentum frame from step 3.1, write $p_1=(E_1,\\mathbf p_*)$ and $p_2=(E_2,-\\mathbf p_*)$, so $E_1+E_2=\\sqrt{s}$. The mass-shell equations give $p_1\\cdot p_2=E_1E_2+p_*^2$ and $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}^2=E_i^2-p_*^2$. Direct expansion yields\n$$(p_1\\cdot p_2)^2-\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}}^2\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}^2=(E_1E_2+p_*^2)^2-(E_1^2-p_*^2)(E_2^2-p_*^2)=p_*^2(E_1+E_2)^2=sp_*^2,$$\nso [F2] gives $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. If the incoming rest energies are equal to $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}$, then $E_1=E_2=\\sqrt{s}/2$ and $p_*^2=s/4-\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$; since $p_*>0$, $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. Combining the phase-space density from step 4.1 with [F2]'s coefficient map yields\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega}=\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "For a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$ and two equal-mass daughters, step 4.1 gives $\\int d\\Phi_2=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(8\\pi)$. The decay coefficient map in [F2] divides $|G_E|^2\\int d\\Phi_2$ by $2\\hbar\\mathsf M_0S_f$; for identical daughters $S_f=2$, this is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$. Its units are $\\mathrm J^2/(\\mathrm{J\\,s\\,J})=\\mathrm s^{-1}$. No survival law enters this coefficient calculation.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
