    "thm-qft-example-massive-tree-annihilation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nWork on a nonempty open coordinate region $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, with coordinates $x^0=ct,x^1,x^2,x^3$ in metres and $\\partial_a=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ for $a\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. Use the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. Here $m\\ge0$ is a mass parameter in kilograms, so $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. The natural-unit field convention below concerns smooth test fields, not pointwise quantum operators. A real scalar field is a map $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$; a Dirac test field is $\\Psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$. Fix dimensionless matrices $\\gamma^a\\in M_4(\\mathbb C)$ satisfying $\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a=2h^{ab}I_4$, and put $\\overline\\Psi=\\Psi^\\dagger\\gamma^0$. Adopt dimensionless action normalizations with kinetic terms $\\tfrac12\\int_U h^{ab}(\\partial_a\\varphi)(\\partial_b\\varphi)\\,d^4x$ and $\\int_U i\\overline\\Psi\\gamma^a\\partial_a\\Psi\\,d^4x$, summing repeated indices from $0$ to $3$. The coefficients are dimensionless and introduce no additional length scale. These conventions assign $[\\varphi]=\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $[\\Psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$: the integrands have length degrees $2[\\varphi]-2$ and $2[\\Psi]-1$, while $d^4x$ has degree $4$. No dynamics or continuum existence assertion is adopted.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, $\\mu_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}=\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,C^{-2}}$, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation take $\\phi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R)$ in volts and $\\mathbf A\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R^3)$ in tesla-metres. Write $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$ for the components of the real one-form $A=\\sum_{a=0}^3 A_a\\,dx^a$, so every $A_a$ is in tesla-metres. Take $\\chi\\in C^2(U;\\mathbb R)$ in webers and a charged test field $\\psi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb C^N)$, where $N\\ge1$ is fixed. With a constant real charge parameter $q$ in coulombs, define $D_a\\psi=\\partial_a\\psi-iqA_a\\psi/\\hbar$ componentwise. The transformed fields are again $C^1$, with $d\\chi=\\sum_a(\\partial_a\\chi)dx^a$. In operator notation the conventions are\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the finite Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], and the separate geometric metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and algebraic contraction $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Write $p=(E,\\mathbf p)$ for an energy four-vector with components in joules, $M\\geq0$ for a rest-energy parameter in joules, and $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+M^2}>0$. In the natural-length variables of the Dirac definition, $\\mathbf k=\\mathbf p/(\\hbar c)$, $\\mu=M/(\\hbar c)$, and $\\omega=E/(\\hbar c)$.\n\nLet $\\beta=\\gamma^0$, $\\alpha_j=\\beta\\gamma^j$, $\\Sigma_j=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_j,\\sigma_j)$, and $\\gamma_5=i\\gamma^0\\gamma^1\\gamma^2\\gamma^3$. Define $H_\\pm=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf p\\pm\\beta M$ and $P_\\pm=(I_4+H_\\pm/E)/2$. Each $P_\\pm$ is a Hermitian rank-two projection. Choose orthonormal bases $\\xi_{\\pm,1},\\xi_{\\pm,2}$ of their ranges and put $u_s=\\sqrt{2E}\\xi_{+,s}$ and $v_s=\\sqrt{2E}\\xi_{-,s}$ for $s=1,2$. With $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$ and $\\not p=\\gamma^0E-\\sum_j\\gamma^jp_j$, the following hold:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}I_4=4,\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}\\gamma^a=0,\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b)=4h^{ab},\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c)=0,$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c\\gamma^d) =4(h^{ab}h^{cd}-h^{ac}h^{bd}+h^{ad}h^{bc}), \\qquad \\sum_s u_s\\bar u_s=\\not p+M,\\quad \\sum_s v_s\\bar v_s=\\not p-M.$$\n\nThe columns satisfy $(\\not p-M)u_s=0$ and $(\\not p+M)v_s=0$. For all $x,y\\in\\mathbb C^4$, complex conjugation of a Dirac current is $\\overline{\\bar x\\gamma^a y}=\\bar y\\gamma^a x$. The Countable Choice hypothesis written in the current draft [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is retained as a supplier assumption; this finite-dimensional proof makes no additional choice use.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. Write\n$$\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^3:E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+\\mathsf M^2}\\}$$\nfor the positive shell in that Definition, and $C_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3:E\\geq|\\mathbf q|\\}$ for the closed future cone of $h=(+,-,-,-)$. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda(C_+)=C_+$. For energy four-vectors on these positive shells, the shell measure $\\nu_{\\mathsf M}$ defined there is invariant under every such map. Let $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$, let $P$ be future timelike, meaning $P^0>0$ and $h(P,P)>0$, set $s=h(P,P)>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$, and write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2}$. For a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda_P$ with $\\Lambda_P P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$, define $\\Phi_{2,P}$ as the pushforward of the angular measure $\\Phi_2$ from that Definition under\n$$\\mathbf n\\longmapsto\\left(\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)\\right),$$\nwhere $E_i^*$ and $k_*$ are from [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The proof below shows that $\\Phi_{2,P}$ is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda_P$; for $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ it is the pushforward $(\\Psi_2)_\\#\\Phi_2$ under the center-of-momentum map $\\Psi_2$ defined there. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, for each fixed angular direction and nonnegative Borel radial function $g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty]$, the radial action after spatial evaluation is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_{2,P}$. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\Phi_{2,P}(\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2})=\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\Phi_{2,P}(\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M})=\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For the scattering flux identities, take incoming $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)\\in\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}}$, where $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, and require $p_1+p_2=P$. A center-of-momentum map is a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$; write $\\Lambda p_1=(E_1^*,\\mathbf p_1^*)$ and define $p_*=|\\mathbf p_1^*|$. The value is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda$, since two such maps differ by a spatial rotation. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest, represented by $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$ with $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$, and two equal-rest-energy daughters on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}$ with total four-vector $P_0$ and constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K(z;\\zeta)=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta)$ and $S(z;\\zeta)$ be the finite-dimensional formal matrices of [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]], with $d\\ge1$, $K_0(\\zeta)$ invertible, and both coefficient matrices covariant under the vertex phase angles of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]]. Then $S$ is a two-sided inverse of $K$ in $M_d(\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket)$, and\n$$ S(z;\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta S(z;\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1} $$\ncoefficientwise. For a real vertex tuple $\\theta$, put\n$\\Theta:=\\operatorname{diag}_{x\\in X}(\\theta_xI_r)$,\n$\\zeta_s:=\\zeta^{s\\theta}$, and $D_s:=D_{s\\theta}=e^{is\\Theta}$.\nWrite $K_s:=K(z;\\zeta_s)$ and $S_s:=S(z;\\zeta_s)$, and define\n$\\delta K:=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}K_s\\right|_{s=0}$ and\n$\\delta S:=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}S_s\\right|_{s=0}$ coefficientwise along this\ndifferentiable phase path. The variations satisfy\n$$ \\delta K=i[\\Theta,K],\\qquad \\delta S=i[\\Theta,S]=-S(\\delta K)S . $$\nFor ordinary finite matrices $A,B\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$, the corresponding resolvent identity is $B^{-1}(B-A)A^{-1}=A^{-1}-B^{-1}$. These are formal or finite algebraic statements; they assert no convergence of a perturbation series, continuum limit, or loop Ward identity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], the spin sums reduce the squared-current factors to\n$$L_e^{ab}=\\frac14\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\not p_2-m)\\gamma^a(\\not p_1+m)\\gamma^b], \\qquad L_{\\mu,ab}=\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\not k_1+M)\\gamma_a(\\not k_2-M)\\gamma_b].$$\nThe odd traces vanish. Expanding the even terms with [F2] and using $p_1\\cdot p_2+m^2=s/2$ and $k_1\\cdot k_2+M^2=s/2$ gives\n$$L_e^{ab}=p_2^ap_1^b+p_2^bp_1^a-\\frac{s}{2}h^{ab}, \\qquad L_{\\mu,ab}=4(k_{1a}k_{2b}+k_{1b}k_{2a}-\\frac{s}{2}h_{ab}).$$\nThese mass-shell dot products follow from $(p_1+p_2)^2=(k_1+k_2)^2=s$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Contracting the tensors gives\n$$L_e^{ab}L_{\\mu,ab} =4\\{2[(p_2\\cdot k_1)(p_1\\cdot k_2)+(p_2\\cdot k_2)(p_1\\cdot k_1)] +s^2-s(p_1\\cdot p_2+k_1\\cdot k_2)\\}.$$\nThe two cross-metric terms contribute $-s(p_1\\cdot p_2+k_1\\cdot k_2)$, while the metric-metric term contributes $s^2$; substituting $p_1\\cdot p_2=(s-2m^2)/2$ and $k_1\\cdot k_2=(s-2M^2)/2$ reduces the last two terms to $s(m^2+M^2)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The finite matrix hypothesis [L1] supplies coefficientwise inverse and phase-covariance identities only for its specified finite graph kernel. For the separate amplitude in [A1], the spinor equations from [F2] give $\\bar v(p_2)\\not p_2=-m\\bar v(p_2)$ and $\\not p_1u(p_1)=mu(p_1)$, so their sum contracted with the incoming current is zero. Likewise $\\bar u(k_1)\\not k_1=M\\bar u(k_1)$ and $\\not k_2v(k_2)=-Mv(k_2)$, so the outgoing current is transverse to $P=k_1+k_2$. A longitudinal propagator term is therefore multiplied by two zero contractions. No broader tree-current claim is taken from [L1].",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "In the center-of-momentum frame, the equal-mass positive-shell formulas of [F3] give $|\\mathbf p_1^*|=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_e/2$ and $|\\mathbf k_1^*|=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_\\mu/2$. By the definition of $\\theta$ in the Statement, the four cross-dot-products in step 2.1 are $s/4\\pm|\\mathbf p_1^*||\\mathbf k_1^*|\\cos\\theta$, with each sign occurring twice. Substitution into step 2.1 and multiplication by $g^4/s^2$ gives $\\overline{|\\mathcal M|^2}=g^4[3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta]$. Each basis spinor $\\xi_{\\pm,s}$ is dimensionless, so $u_s$ and $v_s$ have units $\\mathrm J^{1/2}$; each current has units $\\mathrm J$, and $g^2/s$ has units $\\mathrm J^{-2}$. Thus $\\mathcal M$ is dimensionless as required by the coefficient map. For each outgoing direction $\\mathbf n\\in S^2$, the rank-two projections $P_+$ and $P_-$ of [F2] depend continuously on $\\mathbf n$, since the outgoing energies are positive. For either projection field $P(\\mathbf n)$, the sets on which each fixed pair of columns $P(\\mathbf n)e_j,P(\\mathbf n)e_k$ is linearly independent are open and cover $S^2$: a rank-two matrix has two linearly independent columns. Order the six pairs and partition $S^2$ by the first pair whose columns are independent. On each Borel piece, Gram--Schmidt gives a Borel orthonormal frame of the range. Thus the outgoing spinors can be chosen Borel in $\\mathbf n$; the incoming spinors are fixed. The spin-resolved amplitudes are Borel, and their finite spin-label sums are Borel. The trace formula already computed makes the averaged squared amplitude continuous. [F2] The strict threshold gives positive $\\beta_e,\\beta_\\mu$, and [F3] gives incoming flux $F=2s\\beta_e>0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For distinguishable final particles, [F3] gives $d\\Phi_2/d\\Omega=\\beta_\\mu/(32\\pi^2)$ and $F=2s\\beta_e$. Substituting the squared amplitude from step 3.1 into [F3] yields the displayed differential coefficient after using $g^4=(4\\pi\\alpha)^2$. To integrate, choose the polar axis along $\\mathbf p_1^*$ and write the outgoing unit direction as $X(\\theta,\\varphi)=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\varphi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\varphi,\\cos\\theta)$. The phase-space angular measure is the chart surface measure on $S^2$ and is invariant under rotations by [F3]. This chart has area density $\\sin\\theta$; the omitted meridian and poles have zero chart area. Thus $\\int_{S^2}d\\Omega=2\\pi\\int_0^\\pi\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta=4\\pi$ and $\\int_{S^2}\\cos^2\\theta\\,d\\Omega=2\\pi\\int_0^\\pi\\cos^2\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta=4\\pi/3$. This gives the displayed total coefficient.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For $\\delta=4m^2/s\\leq1/2$, set $B_0=2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta$ and $Q=1-\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta$. Then $B_0\\geq1$, $0\\leq Q\\leq1$, and the ratio of the full coefficient to its $m=0$ value is $R=(B_0+\\delta Q)/(B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta})\\geq1$. Also\n$$R-1=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-\\delta}}-1+ \\frac{\\delta Q}{B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}} \\leq2\\sqrt2\\,\\delta\\leq4\\delta,$$\nbecause $1/\\sqrt{1-\\delta}-1=\\delta/(\\sqrt{1-\\delta}(1+\\sqrt{1-\\delta}))\\leq\\sqrt2\\,\\delta$ for $0\\leq\\delta\\leq1/2$, and $B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}\\geq1/\\sqrt2$. This compares only the two formal tree coefficients.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem has fixed incoming and outgoing spin labels and a nonempty above-threshold shell."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The allowed m=0 and M=0 massless specializations are included in the spin traces and coefficient formula of steps 1.1, 3.1, and 4.1; g is fixed positive by the adopted convention."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
