          "evidence": "The massless limits are included in steps 3.1 and 4.1, delta=0 is included in step 5.1, and the strict threshold remains excluded by the Statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The calculation uses only the finite spin labels and spin sums in step 1.1; it makes no arbitrary-index family selection."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Full-mass unpolarized formal pair-production coefficient: Let $m,M\\geq0$ be the electron and muon rest energies in joules. Let $p_1,p_2$ be future electron/positron energy four-vectors of rest energy $m$, and $k_1,k_2$ future muon/antimuon energy four-vectors of rest energy $M$, with $p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$, $s=P^2>4\\max(m^2,M^2)$, and the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$. Use the dimensionless coupling $g=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}$ from [[def-qft-example-data]]. Adopt only the formal single-photon tree coefficient $$\\mathcal M=\\frac{g^2}{s} [\\bar v(p_2)\\gamma^a u(p_1)] [\\bar u(k_1)\\gamma_a v(k_2)],$$ with equal weight $1/4$ on the four initial spin-label pairs and a sum over final spin labels. No interacting continuum $S$-matrix or exact QED cross section is assumed. In the auxili The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Full-mass unpolarized formal pair-production coefficient: Let $m,M\\geq0$ be the electron and muon rest energies in joules. Let $p_1,p_2$ be future electron/positron energy four-vectors of rest energy $m$, and $k_1,k_2$ future muon/antimuon energy four-vectors of rest energy $M$, with $p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$, $s=P^2>4\\max(m^2,M^2)$, and the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$. Use the dimensionless coupling $g=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}$ from [[def-qft-example-data]]. Adopt only the formal single-photon tree coefficient $$\\mathcal M=\\frac{g^2}{s} [\\bar v(p_2)\\gamma^a u(p_1)] [\\bar u(k_1)\\gamma_a v(k_2)],$$ with equal weight $1/4$ on the four initial spin-label pairs and a sum over final spin labels. No interacting continuum $S$-matrix or exact QED cross section is assumed. In the auxili The reverse iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "Set m=M=0 in the proved differential coefficient and integrate 1+cos^2(theta) over S^2.",
          "result": "The local algebra gives 4 pi alpha^2 (hbar c)^2/(3s); this is a formal coefficient, not data."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-46",
        "notes": "Read all six steps and every F/L supplier at exact finite/measure interfaces, including full matrix-Ward proof and formal/graph/gauge definitions. Trace contraction gives 4{2 cross-products+s(m^2+M^2)}; COM products s/4 ± s beta_e beta_mu cos(theta)/4 give the displayed full-mass polynomial. Finite first-independent-column Borel partition and Gram–Schmidt establish Borel spin-resolved amplitudes; sums are basis independent. Each spinor has J^(1/2) units, amplitude dimensionless. Positive strict threshold makes both momenta and flux nonzero, including m=M=0. Current cancellations follow from both Dirac equations and adjoints, never from a continuum Ward theorem. Integrals 4pi and 4pi/3 give total. With delta<=1/2, B0>=1 and 0<=Q<=1 bound ratio between 1 and 1+4delta, including delta=0 and 1/2. Reader/refuter no findings. Existing readiness remarks are historical workflow qualifications, not proof gaps; no transitive supplier acceptance or empirical prediction is claimed."
      }
    },
    "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. Write\n$$\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^3:E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+\\mathsf M^2}\\}$$\nfor the positive shell in that Definition, and $C_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3:E\\geq|\\mathbf q|\\}$ for the closed future cone of $h=(+,-,-,-)$. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda(C_+)=C_+$. For energy four-vectors on these positive shells, the shell measure $\\nu_{\\mathsf M}$ defined there is invariant under every such map. Let $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$, let $P$ be future timelike, meaning $P^0>0$ and $h(P,P)>0$, set $s=h(P,P)>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$, and write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2}$. For a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda_P$ with $\\Lambda_P P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$, define $\\Phi_{2,P}$ as the pushforward of the angular measure $\\Phi_2$ from that Definition under\n$$\\mathbf n\\longmapsto\\left(\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)\\right),$$\nwhere $E_i^*$ and $k_*$ are from [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The proof below shows that $\\Phi_{2,P}$ is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda_P$; for $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ it is the pushforward $(\\Psi_2)_\\#\\Phi_2$ under the center-of-momentum map $\\Psi_2$ defined there. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, for each fixed angular direction and nonnegative Borel radial function $g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty]$, the radial action after spatial evaluation is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_{2,P}$. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\Phi_{2,P}(\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2})=\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\Phi_{2,P}(\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M})=\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For the scattering flux identities, take incoming $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)\\in\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}}$, where $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, and require $p_1+p_2=P$. A center-of-momentum map is a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$; write $\\Lambda p_1=(E_1^*,\\mathbf p_1^*)$ and define $p_*=|\\mathbf p_1^*|$. The value is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda$, since two such maps differ by a spatial rotation. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest, represented by $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$ with $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$, and two equal-rest-energy daughters on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}$ with total four-vector $P_0$ and constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Under [A1], the phase-space and coefficient-map suppliers apply. Since all four scattering vectors lie on the stated positive $m$-shell and have common total $P$, the equal-mass center-of-momentum formulas in [F1] give $p_*=k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$, where $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4m^2/s}>0$. The flux is $2s\\beta>0$ by [F1], so the coefficient map is defined. Its distinguishable differential coefficient for the constant amplitude has density $(\\hbar c)^2\\lambda^2/(64\\pi^2s)$; the factor $\\beta$ in $d\\Phi_2/d\\Omega$ cancels the incoming $\\beta$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The full labeled sphere counts the two identical final scalars twice, so [F1]'s $S_f=2$ divides the differential coefficient by two. Integrating the constant density over area $4\\pi$ gives $\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2/(32\\pi s)$. For distinguishable species $S_f=1$, so both coefficients are twice as large.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For the parent decay, the equal-daughter phase-space integral is $\\beta_0/(8\\pi)$ with $\\beta_0=\\sqrt{1-4m^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}$. The rate map in [F1] divides by $2\\hbar\\mathsf M_0$ and by $S_f=2$, giving $|G_E|^2\\beta_0/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$. Its units are $\\mathrm J^2/(\\mathrm{J\\,s\\,J})=\\mathrm s^{-1}$. The definition is a rate coefficient only; no time-dependent survival law was assumed.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The scalar amplitude and above-threshold phase space are fixed by the statement.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume Countable Choice, $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ [[def-countable-choice]], as required by the phase-space and coefficient-map suppliers used below.\n\nLet $m\\geq0$ be a scalar rest energy in joules, and let $p_1,p_2,k_1,k_2$ be the incoming and outgoing scattering vectors on the positive $m$-shell of [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]], so each $(E,\\mathbf q)$ satisfies $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+m^2}>0$. Require $p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$ and $s=h(P,P)>4m^2$, with $h$ as in [[def-qft-example-data]]. Evaluate the coefficient map in a center-of-momentum frame $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ as defined in [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. For one scalar initial label of weight $1$ and one specified final channel, take the dimensionless real quartic tree amplitude $\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$. The coefficient map of [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]] gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{128\\pi^2s}, \\qquad \\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{32\\pi s}$$\nfor identical final scalars integrated over the full labeled sphere. For distinguishable final scalars the two coefficients are twice these values.\n\nFor a parent at rest, $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$ with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2m$, decaying through a specified single channel to two identical scalars on the positive $m$-shell whose total four-vector is $P_0$, under a constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}} =\\frac{|G_E|^2}{32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0} \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m^2}{\\mathsf M_0^2}}.$$\nThis result concerns the defined formal coefficients. It does not assert an exact exponential survival law.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "d793f03781c835559b32bcd261df1150b118fec219db3b00d473d840c74f5319",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
