      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-46",
        "notes": "Read steps 1.1–3.1 against phase-space lemma and coefficient definition. Strict s>4m^2 ensures positive flux and cancellation kstar/pstar=1. Full labeled sphere and S_f=2 give 1/(128 pi^2 s) differential and 1/(32 pi s) total; distinguishable channel doubles both. Decay factor beta0/(32 pi hbar M0) has inverse-time units. m=0 and lambda=0 or GE=0 remain valid, parent threshold excluded. Reader/refuter no findings. Only defined formal maps are claimed."
      }
    },
    "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nWork on a nonempty open coordinate region $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, with coordinates $x^0=ct,x^1,x^2,x^3$ in metres and $\\partial_a=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ for $a\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. Use the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. Here $m\\ge0$ is a mass parameter in kilograms, so $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. The natural-unit field convention below concerns smooth test fields, not pointwise quantum operators. A real scalar field is a map $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$; a Dirac test field is $\\Psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$. Fix dimensionless matrices $\\gamma^a\\in M_4(\\mathbb C)$ satisfying $\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a=2h^{ab}I_4$, and put $\\overline\\Psi=\\Psi^\\dagger\\gamma^0$. Adopt dimensionless action normalizations with kinetic terms $\\tfrac12\\int_U h^{ab}(\\partial_a\\varphi)(\\partial_b\\varphi)\\,d^4x$ and $\\int_U i\\overline\\Psi\\gamma^a\\partial_a\\Psi\\,d^4x$, summing repeated indices from $0$ to $3$. The coefficients are dimensionless and introduce no additional length scale. These conventions assign $[\\varphi]=\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $[\\Psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$: the integrands have length degrees $2[\\varphi]-2$ and $2[\\Psi]-1$, while $d^4x$ has degree $4$. No dynamics or continuum existence assertion is adopted.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, $\\mu_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}=\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,C^{-2}}$, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation take $\\phi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R)$ in volts and $\\mathbf A\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R^3)$ in tesla-metres. Write $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$ for the components of the real one-form $A=\\sum_{a=0}^3 A_a\\,dx^a$, so every $A_a$ is in tesla-metres. Take $\\chi\\in C^2(U;\\mathbb R)$ in webers and a charged test field $\\psi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb C^N)$, where $N\\ge1$ is fixed. With a constant real charge parameter $q$ in coulombs, define $D_a\\psi=\\partial_a\\psi-iqA_a\\psi/\\hbar$ componentwise. The transformed fields are again $C^1$, with $d\\chi=\\sum_a(\\partial_a\\chi)dx^a$. In operator notation the conventions are\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $\\psi\\in\\mathcal C$, expand $\\pi_i\\pi_j\\psi-\\pi_j\\pi_i\\psi$. The second derivatives and products $A_iA_j$ cancel; the remaining derivative terms are $iq\\hbar(\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i)\\psi=iq\\hbar\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k\\psi$ by the stated alternating-symbol and summation conventions. Smoothness of $A$ ensures each multiplication and differentiation maps $\\mathcal C$ to itself.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "In $\\mathbb R[X,t]/(t^2)$, the coefficient of $tX$ in $(mc^2+taX)^2$ is $2mc^2a$. The defining identity in the Statement sets this equal to $c^2$, so $a=1/(2m)$ since $m>0$. Substituting $K$ for $X$, the spin part of $K/(2m)$ is $-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B/(2m)=-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Direct multiplication of the displayed $2\\times2$ matrices gives $\\sigma_i^2=I_2$, $\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3$, $\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\sigma_1$, and $\\sigma_3\\sigma_1=i\\sigma_2$; each reversed product has the opposite sign. Thus $\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=\\delta_{ij}I_2+i\\epsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k$. Block multiplication gives $\\alpha_i\\alpha_j=\\delta_{ij}I_4+i\\epsilon_{ijk}\\Sigma_k$, $\\alpha_i\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_i$, and $\\beta^2=I_4$. These fixed constant matrices commute with every $\\pi_j$. Expand $(\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\pi_i)^2=\\sum_{i,j}\\alpha_i\\alpha_j\\pi_i\\pi_j$. The $\\delta_{ij}$ term in the derived block product is the operator $\\boldsymbol\\pi^2$ defined in the Statement. Its antisymmetric term is\n$$\\frac{i}{2}\\epsilon_{ijk}\\Sigma_k[\\pi_i,\\pi_j] =-\\frac{q\\hbar}{2}\\epsilon_{ijk}\\epsilon_{ij\\ell}\\Sigma_kB_\\ell =-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B,$$\nbecause $\\epsilon_{ijk}\\epsilon_{ij\\ell}=2\\delta_{k\\ell}$. The cross terms with $\\beta mc^2$ cancel by $\\alpha_i\\beta+\\beta\\alpha_i=0$, and $\\beta^2=I_4$. This proves the displayed square identity on $\\mathcal C$; no product outside that domain is used.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The coefficient of the term linear in $q$ is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$. Equating it with $-g_s(q/(2m))\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$ as a formal coefficient identity gives $g_s=2$, including the zero-charge specialization of that identity. This is only a formal lowest-order coefficient comparison, not an operator or empirical conclusion.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The operator identity is on the fixed core C_c^infty(R^3;C^4).",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $m>0$ be a particle mass in kilograms, $q\\in\\mathbb R$ its charge in coulombs, and $A:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a smooth spatial vector potential with components in tesla-metres. Use standard Cartesian coordinates $x_i$ measured in metres and write $\\partial_i:=\\partial/\\partial x_i$. Let $\\epsilon_{ijk}$ be the alternating symbol with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, and sum repeated spatial indices from $1$ to $3$. Define $B_k(x)=\\epsilon_{kij}\\partial_iA_j(x)$ and $\\mathbf B=(B_1,B_2,B_3):\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$; its smooth components are in tesla.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$ be the common invariant test domain. In this item, “common core” refers only to this shared domain of the displayed differential expressions; no operator-theoretic core property for a closure is asserted. Define the dimensionless finite matrices\n$$\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},$$\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_i=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_i\\\\\\sigma_i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\Sigma_i=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_i),$$\nand use $\\hbar,c$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. On $\\mathcal C$, define\n$$\\pi_i\\psi=-i\\hbar\\partial_i\\psi-qA_i\\psi,\\qquad H_D\\psi=c\\sum_{i=1}^3\\alpha_i\\pi_i\\psi+\\beta mc^2\\psi.$$\nThese maps preserve $\\mathcal C$. The components $\\pi_i$ have units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $H_D$ has units joules. Define the operator expressions\n$$\\boldsymbol\\pi^2\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\pi_i(\\pi_i\\psi),\\qquad (\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B)\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\Sigma_iB_i\\psi.$$\nHere $[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]:=\\pi_i\\circ\\pi_j-\\pi_j\\circ\\pi_i$, and $H_D^2$ denotes composition on $\\mathcal C$. On $\\mathcal C$, the identities are\n$$[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]=iq\\hbar\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k,\\qquad H_D^2=m^2c^4I_4+c^2(\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B).$$\nThe operator $K:=\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B$ maps $\\mathcal C$ to itself and has units $(\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}})^2$.\n\nFor commuting formal indeterminates $t,X$ over $\\mathbb R$, define the first-order square-root coefficient $a$ by\n$$(mc^2+taX)^2\\equiv m^2c^4+tc^2X\\pmod{t^2}.$$\nComparing the coefficient of $tX$ gives $a=1/(2m)$; substituting $K$ for $X$ gives the formal first-degree correction $K/(2m)$, with no operator square-root assertion. Its spin term is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$, where $S_i=\\hbar\\Sigma_i/2$, $\\mathbf S=(S_1,S_2,S_3)$, and $(\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B)\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3S_iB_i\\psi$. The magnetic-moment dot product below uses the same componentwise sum convention.\n\nUnder the coefficient convention $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}=-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}\\cdot\\mathbf B$ and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}=g_s(q/(2m))\\mathbf S$, equality of the formal coefficients as a linear function of $q$ gives $g_s=2$ for the minimal tree-level term. Here $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}$ has units joules and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}$ has units $\\mathrm{A\\,m^2}$. No magnetic self-adjoint closure, full spectrum, operator square-root theorem, radiative correction, or empirical prediction is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f61bdfe2b0f57bba48cdb8b7af7b7623f0eb0ded169f5731a0e995bf00627398",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
