    "ex-qftmath-x04": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-qft-example-massive-tree-annihilation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,M\\geq0$ be the electron and muon rest energies in joules. Use the positive shells of [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]: $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)$ with $E_i=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p_i|^2+m^2}>0$ for $i=1,2$, and $k_i=(\\varepsilon_i,\\mathbf k_i)$ with $\\varepsilon_i=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k_i|^2+M^2}>0$ for $i=1,2$. With $p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$, set $s=h(P,P)>4\\max(m^2,M^2)$, using the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Since $P^0=E_1+E_2>0$ and $h(P,P)=s>0$, $P$ is future timelike. A center-of-momentum map is a proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, $\\Lambda(C_+)=C_+$ for $C_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3:E\\geq|\\mathbf q|\\}$, and $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$. Write $\\mathbf p_1^*$ and $\\mathbf k_1^*$ for the spatial components of $\\Lambda p_1$ and $\\Lambda k_1$, respectively. The strict threshold makes both nonzero; define $\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]$ by $\\cos\\theta=(\\mathbf p_1^*\\cdot_{\\mathbb R^3}\\mathbf k_1^*)/(|\\mathbf p_1^*||\\mathbf k_1^*|)$. This angle is independent of the chosen center-of-momentum map because any two choices differ by a spatial rotation. For $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, set $h_{ab}=h^{ab}$ equal to the diagonal entries of $h=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$, sum repeated Lorentz indices over this range, and define $\\gamma_a:=\\sum_{b=0}^3h_{ab}\\gamma^b$ and $P_a:=\\sum_{b=0}^3h_{ab}P^b$. Use the dimensionless coupling $g=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}$ from [[def-qft-example-data]]. Adopt only the formal single-photon tree coefficient\n$$\\mathcal M=\\frac{g^2}{s} [\\bar v(p_2)\\gamma^a u(p_1)] [\\bar u(k_1)\\gamma_a v(k_2)],$$\nwith equal weight $1/4$ on the four initial spin-label pairs and a sum over final spin labels. The outgoing labels denote the distinct $\\mu^-$ and $\\mu^+$ species, so $S_f=1$ in the coefficient map. With the spinor normalization of [[lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness]], $\\mathcal M$ is dimensionless. No interacting continuum $S$-matrix or exact QED cross section is assumed.\n\nIn the auxiliary finite-matrix setting of [L1], assume $d\\geq1$, an invertible $K_0$, and the stated covariance of $K_0$ and $V$ under the finite-graph phase action; then the coefficientwise inverse and covariance identities in [L1] hold. Separately stipulate the displayed formal pair-production amplitude above. Its on-shell spin average is\n$$\\overline{|\\mathcal M|^2} =g^4[3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2 +\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta], \\qquad \\beta_e=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m^2}{s}}, \\quad \\beta_\\mu=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{4M^2}{s}},$$\nwhere the angle $\\theta$ is defined above. The resulting formal differential and total coefficients are\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2\\beta_\\mu}{4s\\beta_e} [3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta],$$\n$$\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}} =\\frac{\\pi\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2}{s}\\frac{\\beta_\\mu}{\\beta_e} [3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\tfrac13\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2].$$\nIf $\\delta=4m^2/s\\leq1/2$, the full differential coefficient divided by its $m=0$ value is between $1$ and $1+4\\delta$. This bound concerns only omission of the incoming electron mass in this formal tree coefficient, not loop, soft-radiation, threshold, or empirical errors.\n\nThe same on-shell equations give $P_a\\bar v(p_2)\\gamma^a u(p_1)=0$ and $P_a\\bar u(k_1)\\gamma^a v(k_2)=0$. Thus a propagator term proportional to $P_aP_b$ does not change this specified tree coefficient. These current cancellations are proved locally for all $m,M\\geq0$; the actual QFT-MG03 matrix-Ward carrier supplies only its finite-matrix inverse and covariance interface, not the broader tree-current clauses in the historical Q7 research module.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Setting $\\beta_e=\\beta_\\mu=1$ in [F1] gives $d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}/d\\Omega=\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2(1+\\cos^2\\theta)/(4s)$. At $\\theta=0$ this is $\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2/(2s)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2/(4s)$, so the ratio is $2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Integrating the same density over the sphere uses $\\int d\\Omega=4\\pi$ and $\\int\\cos^2\\theta\\,d\\Omega=4\\pi/3$, giving $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=4\\pi\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2/(3s)$, in agreement with [F1]. No count data or continuum scattering operator is introduced.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
