    "ex-qftmath-x09": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m>0$ be a particle mass in kilograms, $q\\in\\mathbb R$ its charge in coulombs, and $A:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a smooth spatial vector potential with components in tesla-metres. Use standard Cartesian coordinates $x_i$ measured in metres and write $\\partial_i:=\\partial/\\partial x_i$. Let $\\epsilon_{ijk}$ be the alternating symbol with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, and sum repeated spatial indices from $1$ to $3$. Define $B_k(x)=\\epsilon_{kij}\\partial_iA_j(x)$ and $\\mathbf B=(B_1,B_2,B_3):\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$; its smooth components are in tesla.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$ be the common invariant test domain. In this item, “common core” refers only to this shared domain of the displayed differential expressions; no operator-theoretic core property for a closure is asserted. Define the dimensionless finite matrices\n$$\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},$$\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_i=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_i\\\\\\sigma_i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\Sigma_i=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_i),$$\nand use $\\hbar,c$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. On $\\mathcal C$, define\n$$\\pi_i\\psi=-i\\hbar\\partial_i\\psi-qA_i\\psi,\\qquad H_D\\psi=c\\sum_{i=1}^3\\alpha_i\\pi_i\\psi+\\beta mc^2\\psi.$$\nThese maps preserve $\\mathcal C$. The components $\\pi_i$ have units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $H_D$ has units joules. Define the operator expressions\n$$\\boldsymbol\\pi^2\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\pi_i(\\pi_i\\psi),\\qquad (\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B)\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\Sigma_iB_i\\psi.$$\nHere $[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]:=\\pi_i\\circ\\pi_j-\\pi_j\\circ\\pi_i$, and $H_D^2$ denotes composition on $\\mathcal C$. On $\\mathcal C$, the identities are\n$$[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]=iq\\hbar\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k,\\qquad H_D^2=m^2c^4I_4+c^2(\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B).$$\nThe operator $K:=\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B$ maps $\\mathcal C$ to itself and has units $(\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}})^2$.\n\nFor commuting formal indeterminates $t,X$ over $\\mathbb R$, define the first-order square-root coefficient $a$ by\n$$(mc^2+taX)^2\\equiv m^2c^4+tc^2X\\pmod{t^2}.$$\nComparing the coefficient of $tX$ gives $a=1/(2m)$; substituting $K$ for $X$ gives the formal first-degree correction $K/(2m)$, with no operator square-root assertion. Its spin term is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$, where $S_i=\\hbar\\Sigma_i/2$, $\\mathbf S=(S_1,S_2,S_3)$, and $(\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B)\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3S_iB_i\\psi$. The magnetic-moment dot product below uses the same componentwise sum convention.\n\nUnder the coefficient convention $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}=-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}\\cdot\\mathbf B$ and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}=g_s(q/(2m))\\mathbf S$, equality of the formal coefficients as a linear function of $q$ gives $g_s=2$ for the minimal tree-level term. Here $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}$ has units joules and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}$ has units $\\mathrm{A\\,m^2}$. No magnetic self-adjoint closure, full spectrum, operator square-root theorem, radiative correction, or empirical prediction is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "With $q=-e$, the supplier's term is $(e/m)S_3B_0$. Since $\\sigma_3$ has eigenvalues $\\pm1$, $S_3$ has eigenvalues $\\pm\\hbar/2$, so the two coefficients are $\\pm e\\hbar B_0/(2m)=\\pm\\mu_BB_0$, where $\\mu_B=e\\hbar/(2m)$. Their gap is $e\\hbar B_0/m$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Dividing that formal energy gap by $\\hbar$ gives the angular-frequency coefficient $\\omega_s=eB_0/m$ in $\\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$. The cyclic-frequency coefficient is $\\nu_s:=\\omega_s/(2\\pi)=eB_0/(2\\pi m)$ in hertz, so $\\nu_s=\\nu_{\\rm comp}$ and $\\omega_s=\\omega_{\\rm comp}$. This compares formal coefficients only; it is not a full Landau-spectrum result or an empirical prediction.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
